# Tessera source must use Unix line endings: the lexer reports E0101 for a
# carriage return. The shell scripts, the Rust runtime and the generated
# stage1/runtime_text.tes are LF for the same reason, so the repository is
# normalised to LF everywhere. A Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true then
# still builds and the `.out` files still match byte for byte.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.gif  binary
*.webm binary
*.bin  binary
# Never committed (decision D5), but if one ever is, it must not be rewritten.
*.wad  binary
*.sav  binary

# What GitHub, and the code datasets that follow it, should count as Tessera.
# Three files are machine-written and would teach a reader (or a model) the
# wrong thing about the language:
#   runtime_text.tes   the Rust runtime escaped into Tessera string literals
#   tessera1.rs        the generated seed, a megabyte of generated Rust
#   harness/corpora    snapshots of older compiler drafts, kept to measure
#                      token cost, so near-duplicates of stage1/ by design
stage1/runtime_text.tes   linguist-generated=true
bootstrap/tessera1.rs     linguist-generated=true
harness/corpora/**        linguist-vendored=true
